<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro</h1><p class="st-meta">1567 CE – 1821 CE · capital Rio de Janeiro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The royal captaincy founded after the expulsion of the French from Guanabara Bay, which grew into the capital of Brazil and seat of a European monarchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Portuguese forces drove the French of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/france-antarctique">France Antarctique</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a>, the crown established the Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1567 as a royal rather than hereditary captaincy. The city of São Sebastião do Rio de Janeiro, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estacio-de-sa">Estácio de Sá</a> in 1565, was moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morro-do-castelo">Morro do Castelo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio</a> grew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and as a port for trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">River Plate</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>. The Sá family, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-correia-de-sa-e-benevides">Salvador Correia de Sá</a>, dominated its politics in the 17th century. With the gold rush in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> from the 1690s, Rio became the main outlet for gold and the main entry for enslaved Africans. French corsairs sacked it in 1711. In 1763 it replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> as capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-brazil">State of Brazil</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1808 the Portuguese royal family arrived, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio</a> the capital of the Portuguese empire and, from 1815, of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a>. In 1821 the captaincy became a province, and in 1822 Rio was the capital of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-rio-de-janeiro.jpg" alt="Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Tonyjeff, based on ancient national symbol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_of_Portugal,_Brazil,_and_the_Algarves.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1567 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rio de Janeiro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Salvador Correia de Sá, Gomes Freire de Andrade</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rio de Janeiro captaincy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=captaincy-of-rio-de-janeiro" aria-label="Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3525 3023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3845 3279L3944 2917M3845 3279L3847 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0M3847 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3845 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3845 3279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rio de Janeiro. <a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=captaincy-of-rio-de-janeiro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
